{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Speed isn't free — esbuild's plugin API is real but narrower</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      esbuild plugins only get two real extension points, <code>onResolve</code> and <code>onLoad</code>, hooking how a module is found and what content or loader is used for it. They can't reach into esbuild's own internal transform the way a webpack loader or Babel plugin can rewrite arbitrary JS, which rules out some tooling, certain codegen-heavy, macro-style plugins among them, that has no direct esbuild equivalent yet.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      webpack's decade-plus head start shows up as ecosystem maturity: Module Federation for micro-frontends, incremental and persistent build caching, and a large catalog of existing loaders and plugins for nearly every framework and asset type a team might already have config-hours invested in. Swapping the dev server doesn't require throwing that investment away, most real adoptions, Vite's default setup among them, keep esbuild for fast dev-time transforms and dependency pre-bundling while using Rollup or webpack for the production bundle, where the plugin ecosystem and code-splitting maturity matter more than raw speed.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Worth being precise about what's actually being compared: esbuild alone is a bundler and transpiler, not a dev server with HMR or a plugin lifecycle equivalent to webpack's. Most real "esbuild vs webpack for dev speed" comparisons are actually comparing a tool built on esbuild, Vite, or webpack with its transform step swapped to esbuild-loader, against a from-scratch webpack plus babel-loader plus ts-loader config, not esbuild's bare CLI against webpack's dev server directly. That's a fair comparison of dev-loop speed, but it's closer to "esbuild's transform step replaces the slowest part of a typical webpack config" than "esbuild replaces webpack."
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

